    "product_id": "c90bff8d-c774-41e0-b0fa-e820391ef43c",
    "text": "\n000\nFXUS65 KPSR 120945\nAFDPSR\n\nArea Forecast Discussion\nNational Weather Service Phoenix AZ\n245 AM MST Thu Nov 12 2026\n\n.KEY MESSAGES...\n\n- High pressure will continue to maintain dry and tranquil\n  conditions and above normal temperatures in place through today.\n\n- A more unsettled pattern is expected starting Friday and lasting\n  well into next week with occasional rain chances and cooler \n  temperatures.\n  \n&&\n\n.SHORT TERM /TODAY THROUGH FRIDAY/... \nLatest RAP40 500MB analysis continues to show high pressure \nlingering over the Desert SW region. This will lead to one more \nday of afternoon highs 5F-8F above normal, and dry conditions. By \nlate tonight heading into Friday morning a cut off low will break \noff from the main low pressure system in the Pacific NW and begin \nto progress towards the lower deserts. This low will lower \nafternoon temperatures into the upper 70s Friday. Additionally, \nwith this cut off low inbound and influx of moisture will be \nadvected into the region, going from near to slightly above normal\ntoday (~0.50\") to between 180-200% of normal (0.80\"-0.95\"). \nIsolated showers will be likely as early as Friday afternoon, with\nactivity mostly in southern to southeastern AZ as the core of the\ncut off low tracks along the Arizona/Mexico border. However as \nthe low moves rain chances will be pulled into south-central AZ \nand into the eastern higher terrain Friday afternoon/evening with \nPoPs between 15-30%. While most activity will be light showers, an\nisolated thunderstorm is not out of the question, but total \nrainfall amounts will only amount to a few hundredths for any the\nlower deserts to at maybe 0.10\" in higher terrain areas. \n\n&&\n\n.LONG TERM /SATURDAY THROUGH WEDNESDAY/...\nBy Saturday a second follow-on shortwave trough will quickly move\nin from the northwest as early as sunrise Saturday morning, \nlasting through at least Saturday afternoon. Compared to the short\nwave on Friday, the track of this next disturbance will remain \nacross northern AZ, bringing rain chances to at least the northern\nhalf of Arizona with more doubt for the Arizona lower deserts. \nSimilarly to Friday, rainfall amounts will again be light, \nhowever slightly higher in comparison, especially in the higher \nterrain where amounts between 0.10\"-0.25\" are likely.\n\nBy Saturday evening, the shortwave is expected to push to our\neast quickly decreasing rain chances and likely ending them all\ntogether by around midnight with Sunday slotted to be a down day \nfor shower activity with a brief period of shortwave ridging and \nsome slight dry air advection from the northwest and should lead \nto considerably less clouds and virtually no rain chances.\nTemperatures will continue to be at to slightly below normal over\nthe weekend and heading into next week. \n\nModels continue to forecast one after another northern Pacific \nwaves being ejected southeastward toward the U.S. West Coast with \nthe next potential system reaching the California coast early \nMonday before reaching our region Monday night into Tuesday. This \nmay even be followed by another system during the second half of \nnext week. However, it is too early to have any good sense on the \ntrack of these systems as they could move directly through the \nDesert Southwest or mostly miss us to the north, so continued \nmonitoring will be needed. NBM/WPC PoPs of 20-60% are still in \nthe forecast for the Tuesday disturbance, but that could easily \nchange depending on the eventual track. So far, any system that \nmay move through is not expected to carry anything more than \naverage moisture, so any rainfall amounts are likely to be on the \nlighter side. However, the expected northwestward trajectory of \nthe weather systems next week should usher in even cooler \ntemperatures with highs trending more toward the upper 60s to \nlower 70s during the middle part of the week. \n\n&&\n\n.AVIATION...Updated at 0520Z.\n\nSouth Central Arizona including KPHX, KIWA, KSDL, and KDVT:\nNo major aviation weather concerns are expected through the TAF\nperiod under mostly clear skies. Winds will retain an easterly\ncomponent overnight and through the day tomorrow. After sunrise\ntomorrow morning, marginal breeziness is expected to develop with\ngusts reaching into the teens to around 20 kts at all terminals.\nThis breeziness should persist for a few hours before winds\ngradually relax through the afternoon.\n\nSoutheast California/Southwest Arizona including KIPL and KBLH:\nNo aviation weather concerns are expected over the next 24 hours.\nWinds will continue to favor a westerly component at KIPL and a\nnortherly component at KBLH through tomorrow morning before\nsettling into more of an easterly component at both terminals by\nthe afternoon. Speeds should remain aob 7 kt with extended periods\nof calm and VRB conditions. Mostly clear skies will continue over\nthe region before high clouds begin to move in later in the period.\n\n&&\n\n.FIRE WEATHER...\nThe dry conditions and above normal temperatures will persist\nthrough the rest of today before chances for mostly light rain \nbegin Friday. Minimum humidity values through today will range \nbetween 20-30% for the lower deserts to 30-40% for higher terrain \nareas, while overnight recoveries stay good to excellent at \n40-70%. Expect overall light winds, but also some marginally \nbreezy conditions across ridge tops and terrain gaps of eastern \ndistricts during the morning hours. Winds will increase starting \nFriday, but gusts should mostly stay under 25 mph. Several passing\nweak weather disturbances will bring occasional showers and maybe\nan isolated thunderstorm Friday and Saturday and potentially \nagain early next week. \n\n&&\n\n.HYDROLOGY...\n\nGila: A Flood Warning remains in effect from Painted Rock Dam \nthrough the Dateland area through at least early next week due to \nwater released from the dam. Water release levels are expected to \nincrease during the next couple of days, peaking at 3000 cfs. \nThese increased releases will have the potential to flood farm \nfields and low-water crossings along the river downstream from the\ndam.\n\n&&\n\n.PSR WATCHES/WARNINGS/ADVISORIES...\nAZ...None.\nCA...None.\n&&\n\n$$\n\nSHORT TERM...Ryan\nLONG TERM...Kuhlman/Ryan\nAVIATION...Salerno \nFIRE WEATHER...Kuhlman/Ryan \nHYDROLOGY...Lojero\n",
